{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-68105" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x){e}^{-2x}+3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979528/state.json" "file_sha256" "f97ebb35c9ded11764c24775ea40d6e1b027032bf5d2b5687e09dc5342b7fa5c"} {"question" "q-68105" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that we can rewrite the first term as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x){e}^{-2x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{{e}^{2x}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{2x}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{{e}^{2x}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x){e}^{-2x}+3) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{{e}^{2x}}+3) \\\\  & =0+3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979528/state.json" "file_sha256" "f97ebb35c9ded11764c24775ea40d6e1b027032bf5d2b5687e09dc5342b7fa5c"} {"question" "q-68105" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979528/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "81d70be05185616e84b774f3e9efcc4104ff52e3e944d1276d790174373a6378"} {"question" "q-68105" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979528/state.json" "file_sha256" "f97ebb35c9ded11764c24775ea40d6e1b027032bf5d2b5687e09dc5342b7fa5c"} {"question" "q-68105" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "\\infty "] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979528/state.json" "file_sha256" "f97ebb35c9ded11764c24775ea40d6e1b027032bf5d2b5687e09dc5342b7fa5c"} {"question" "q-68105" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979528/state.json" "file_sha256" "f97ebb35c9ded11764c24775ea40d6e1b027032bf5d2b5687e09dc5342b7fa5c"} {"question" "q-78026" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8x)}+6)=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979528/state.json" "file_sha256" "f97ebb35c9ded11764c24775ea40d6e1b027032bf5d2b5687e09dc5342b7fa5c"} {"question" "q-78026" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${x}^{5}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8x)$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{5}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8x)}=\\infty \n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8x)}+6)=\\infty \n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979528/state.json" "file_sha256" "f97ebb35c9ded11764c24775ea40d6e1b027032bf5d2b5687e09dc5342b7fa5c"} {"question" "q-78026" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979528/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "81d70be05185616e84b774f3e9efcc4104ff52e3e944d1276d790174373a6378"} {"question" "q-78026" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979528/state.json" "file_sha256" "f97ebb35c9ded11764c24775ea40d6e1b027032bf5d2b5687e09dc5342b7fa5c"} {"question" "q-78026" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{49}{8}"] ["math" "\\infty "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979528/state.json" "file_sha256" "f97ebb35c9ded11764c24775ea40d6e1b027032bf5d2b5687e09dc5342b7fa5c"} {"question" "q-78026" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\infty "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979528/state.json" "file_sha256" "f97ebb35c9ded11764c24775ea40d6e1b027032bf5d2b5687e09dc5342b7fa5c"} {"question" "q-78035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x){e}^{-2x}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979528/state.json" "file_sha256" "f97ebb35c9ded11764c24775ea40d6e1b027032bf5d2b5687e09dc5342b7fa5c"} {"question" "q-78035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that we can rewrite the first term as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x){e}^{-2x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{{e}^{2x}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{2x}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{{e}^{2x}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x){e}^{-2x}) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{{e}^{2x}}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979528/state.json" "file_sha256" "f97ebb35c9ded11764c24775ea40d6e1b027032bf5d2b5687e09dc5342b7fa5c"} {"question" "q-78035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979528/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "81d70be05185616e84b774f3e9efcc4104ff52e3e944d1276d790174373a6378"} {"question" "q-78035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979528/state.json" "file_sha256" "f97ebb35c9ded11764c24775ea40d6e1b027032bf5d2b5687e09dc5342b7fa5c"} {"question" "q-78035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{{e}^{2}}"] ["math" "\\infty "] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3"] ["math" "e\\operatorname{ln}⁡3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979528/state.json" "file_sha256" "f97ebb35c9ded11764c24775ea40d6e1b027032bf5d2b5687e09dc5342b7fa5c"} {"question" "q-78035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979528/state.json" "file_sha256" "f97ebb35c9ded11764c24775ea40d6e1b027032bf5d2b5687e09dc5342b7fa5c"} {"question" "q-106830" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3-2x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979528/state.json" "file_sha256" "f97ebb35c9ded11764c24775ea40d6e1b027032bf5d2b5687e09dc5342b7fa5c"} {"question" "q-106830" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rewrite the limit as\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3-2x}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2x-3}.\n$$\n\nThe numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n|-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x|\\le 5.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator $2x-3$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ So the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3-2x}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2x-3}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3-2x},$ shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979528/assets/ab3f7928be50c25689a99751cd180e585d749bf0a5c253c8662f9b820bbf4bb7.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979528/state.json" "file_sha256" "f97ebb35c9ded11764c24775ea40d6e1b027032bf5d2b5687e09dc5342b7fa5c"} {"question" "q-106830" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979528/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "81d70be05185616e84b774f3e9efcc4104ff52e3e944d1276d790174373a6378"} {"question" "q-106830" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979528/state.json" "file_sha256" "f97ebb35c9ded11764c24775ea40d6e1b027032bf5d2b5687e09dc5342b7fa5c"} {"question" "q-106830" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "\\infty "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979528/state.json" "file_sha256" "f97ebb35c9ded11764c24775ea40d6e1b027032bf5d2b5687e09dc5342b7fa5c"} {"question" "q-106830" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979528/state.json" "file_sha256" "f97ebb35c9ded11764c24775ea40d6e1b027032bf5d2b5687e09dc5342b7fa5c"} {"question" "q-338817" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979528/state.json" "file_sha256" "f97ebb35c9ded11764c24775ea40d6e1b027032bf5d2b5687e09dc5342b7fa5c"} {"question" "q-338817" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with \n\n$$\n|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|\\le 1.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator $x$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ Since the denominator grows much faster than the (bounded) numerator, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x},$ shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979528/assets/e164233abcdf9062fd856a100fb7b792912fa030746a63f01ca7fd6683bf7f09.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979528/state.json" "file_sha256" "f97ebb35c9ded11764c24775ea40d6e1b027032bf5d2b5687e09dc5342b7fa5c"} {"question" "q-338817" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979528/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "81d70be05185616e84b774f3e9efcc4104ff52e3e944d1276d790174373a6378"} {"question" "q-338817" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979528/state.json" "file_sha256" "f97ebb35c9ded11764c24775ea40d6e1b027032bf5d2b5687e09dc5342b7fa5c"} {"question" "q-338817" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979528/state.json" "file_sha256" "f97ebb35c9ded11764c24775ea40d6e1b027032bf5d2b5687e09dc5342b7fa5c"}] "usage" {} "basis" 854 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
